        ["oda-nobunaga","Oda Nobunaga","1534–1582","Owari Province, Japan","Kyoto, Japan","Daimyō","Oda clan forces","Nobunaga rose from regional lord to one of the most powerful military leaders of Japan's Sengoku period.","He used alliances, firearms, and campaigns to extend his control; he died during the Honnō-ji incident.","His unification campaigns laid groundwork for the later unification of Japan.","japan-comanders/oda-nabounaga.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Oda-Nobunaga"],
        ["tokugawa-ieyasu","Tokugawa Ieyasu","1543–1616","Okazaki, Mikawa Province, Japan","Sunpu, Japan","Shōgun","Tokugawa clan; Japan","Ieyasu built power through shifting alliances during the Sengoku period.","He won at Sekigahara in 1600, became shōgun in 1603, and established the Tokugawa shogunate.","His government began a long period of Tokugawa rule and political stability.","japan-comanders/tokugawa-leyasu.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Tokugawa-Ieyasu"],
        ["isoroku-yamamoto","Isoroku Yamamoto","1884–1943","Nagaoka, Japan","Bougainville Island","Admiral; commander in chief, Combined Fleet","Imperial Japanese Navy","Yamamoto trained at the Japanese Naval Academy and served in the Russo-Japanese War and overseas postings.","He directed the Combined Fleet during the opening phase of the Pacific War and was killed when his aircraft was intercepted in 1943.","A central Japanese naval commander whose career and decisions are studied in the history of the Pacific War.","japan-comanders/isoroku-yamamoto.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Yamamoto-Isoroku"]
