        ["miltiades","Miltiades","c. 550–489 BCE","Athens (ancient sources vary)","Athens","Athenian general","Athens","Miltiades was an Athenian commander and politician during the Persian Wars.","He is chiefly associated with the Greek victory over the Persian force at Marathon in 490 BCE.","Remembered as a key commander at Marathon; birth year and some biographical details are estimates.","ancient-greece-comanders/mitiades.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Miltiades-Greek-general"],
        ["leonidas-i","Leonidas I","c. 540–480 BCE","Sparta (exact date uncertain)","Thermopylae, Greece","King of Sparta","Spartan Army","Leonidas ruled Sparta during the Persian invasion of Greece.","He led the Greek defense at Thermopylae in 480 BCE and was killed in the battle.","His stand at Thermopylae became a lasting symbol in accounts of Greek resistance; birth year is approximate.","ancient-greece-comanders/leonidas-I.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Leonidas-I"],
        ["themistocles","Themistocles","c. 524–459 BCE","Athens (approximate)","Magnesia, Persian Empire","Athenian general and statesman","Athens","Themistocles promoted Athenian naval power before the Persian invasion.","He helped organize the Greek fleet's victory at Salamis in 480 BCE and later lived in Persian territory.","His naval strategy shaped the balance of power in classical Greece; dates are approximate.","ancient-greece-comanders/themistocles.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Themistocles-Greek-statesman"],
        ["pericles","Pericles","c. 495–429 BCE","Athens (approximate)","Athens","Athenian general and statesman","Athens","Pericles was a leading Athenian politician during the city's classical period.","He held repeated military commands and led Athens through the early years of the Peloponnesian War.","His era saw major cultural projects, including the Parthenon; his birth year is approximate.","ancient-greece-comanders/pericles.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Pericles-Athenian-statesman"],
        ["epaminondas","Epaminondas","c. 418–362 BCE","Thebes (approximate)","Mantinea, Greece","Theban general and statesman","Thebes","Epaminondas rose to prominence as Thebes challenged Spartan dominance in Greece.","He helped secure victory at Leuctra in 371 BCE and died at Mantinea in 362 BCE.","His tactics and campaigns temporarily made Thebes the leading Greek power; birth year is approximate.","ancient-greece-comanders/epaminondas.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Epaminondas"],
        ["alexander-the-great","Alexander the Great","356–323 BCE","Pella, Macedon","Babylon","King of Macedon; military commander","Macedonian Army; Corinthian League","Alexander was educated by Aristotle and succeeded his father Philip II as king of Macedon.","He defeated the Persian Empire and campaigned as far east as the Indus Valley.","His conquests created a vast empire and spread Hellenistic culture across western Asia and Egypt.","ancient-greece-comanders/alezander-the-great.jpg","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Alexander-the-Great"]
